\section*{Solutions}
\subsubsection*{Problem 1}
\begin{solution}
\[S_N(x) = a_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{N}(a_k \cos(kx) + b_k \sin(kx))\]
Starting with \(f(x)=x^2\) on \([-\pi,\pi]\), we notice that \(x^2\) is even so we only need cosine terms.
The constant term is
\[a_0=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}x^2dx
=\frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{2\pi^3}{3}
=\frac{\pi^2}{3}\]
Then we have
\[a_k=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}x^2\cos(kx)dx\]
\[b_k=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}x^2\sin(kx)dx\]
Since \(x^2\sin(kx)\) is odd, \(b_k=0\).
Since \(x^2\cos(kx)\) is even,
\[a_k=\frac{2}{\pi}\int_0^\pi x^2\cos(kx)dx\]
Using integration by parts, we get that
\[\int_0^\pi x^2\cos(kx)dx
=-\frac{2}{k}\int_0^\pi x\sin(kx)dx
=\frac{2\pi(-1)^k}{k^2}\]
so
\[a_k=\frac{2}{\pi}\frac{2\pi(-1)^k}{k^2}
=\frac{4(-1)^k}{k^2}\]
Therefore
\[S_N(x)=\frac{\pi^2}{3}
+4\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{(-1)^k}{k^2}\cos(kx)\] 
For $N=1,2,5,7$, the curves get close to $x^2$ on $[-\pi,\pi]$.
