\end{center}
Over $[-2\pi,2\pi]$, the series repeats the graph on $[-\pi,\pi]$.
\begin{center}
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{hw04_problem1_periodic_plot.png}
\end{center}
\end{solution}
\subsubsection*{Problem 2}
\begin{solution}
The interval has length \(2\), so the cosine and sine terms are
\(\cos(k\pi x)\) and \(\sin(k\pi x)\). Since $f$ is even, $b_k=0$.
The constant term is
\[
a_0=\frac12\int_{-1}^{1}f(x)\,dx
=\frac12\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}1\,dx=\frac12.
\]
For \(k\geq1\), we get
\[
a_k=\int_{-1}^{1}f(x)\cos(k\pi x)\,dx
=\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}\cos(k\pi x)\,dx.
\]
Integrating gives
\[
a_k=\left.\frac{\sin(k\pi x)}{k\pi}\right|_{-1/2}^{1/2}
=\frac{2\sin(k\pi/2)}{k\pi}.
\]
Let $F$ be the sum of the Fourier series. Then $F$ and its partial
sum are
\[
F(x)=\frac12+\frac2\pi\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
\frac{\sin(k\pi/2)}{k}\cos(k\pi x),\qquad
S_N(x)=\frac12+\frac2\pi\sum_{k=1}^{N}
\frac{\sin(k\pi/2)}{k}\cos(k\pi x).
\]
Since $\sin(k\pi/2)=0$ when $k$ is even, we only have odd values of $k$ in our sum.

At $x=\pm1/2$, the graph jumps from $1$ to $0$. Since the partial
sums are continuous, around the jumps, they overshoot and then trace down and it
converges more slowly than problem 1, because of the $\frac{1}{k}$ instead of
$\frac{1}{k^2}$ that shrinks the terms.
\begin{center}
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{hw04_problem2_plot.png}
\end{center}
\end{solution}
\subsubsection*{Problem 3}
\begin{solution}
For the even extension, reflect $f$ across $0$ by setting
$g(x)=f(|x|)$ on $[-a,a]$, then repeat this with period $2a$.
The only places to check are where the pieces meet.

At $0$, both sides approach $f(0)$. At the ends of a period,
\[
g(-a)=f(a)=g(a).
\]
So the copies fit together there too, and the even periodic extension is
continuous everywhere.

For the odd extension, reflect with a minus sign:
$h(x)=-f(-x)$ on $[-a,0)$ and $h(x)=f(x)$ on $(0,a]$.

At $0$, the two sides approach $-f(0)$ and $f(0)$. They agree only if
$f(0)=0$.

At the ends of a period, the two sides approach $-f(a)$ and $f(a)$.
They agree only if $f(a)=0$. If both conditions hold, the pieces fit
together at every join. Thus the odd extension is continuous everywhere
exactly when $f(0)=f(a)=0$.
\end{solution}
\subsubsection*{Problem 4}
\begin{solution}
This is the same function as in Problem 2. We want the value of its Fourier
series $F(x)$ at every point of $[-1,1]$. The function is $1$ between
$-1/2$ and $1/2$, and $0$ outside, so the main question is what happens
at the two jumps.

At a jump, the Fourier series lands halfway between the values on either
side. Here that is $(0+1)/2=1/2$ at both $x=-1/2$ and $x=1/2$.
Everywhere else, the sum agrees with $f$. Therefore
\[
F(x)=
\begin{cases}
1, & -\frac12<x<\frac12,\\[3pt]
\frac12, & x=-\frac12\text{ or }x=\frac12,\\[3pt]
0, & -1\leq x<-\frac12\text{ or }\frac12<x\leq1.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{solution}
\subsubsection*{Problem 5}
\begin{solution}
When we repeat $x^2$ every $2\pi$, the copies meet at the same height:
$(-\pi)^2=\pi^2$. The slope changes where the copies meet, but the graph
does not jump. So the periodic extension is continuous and piecewise smooth,
and its Fourier series converges uniformly to $x^2$ on $[-\pi,\pi]$.

Now set \(x=\pi\). Since \(\cos(k\pi)=(-1)^k\), it cancels the $(-1)^k$
in the coefficient from Problem 1. Plugging this into that series and solving
gives
\[
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1{k^2}
=\frac{\pi^2-\pi^2/3}{4}
=\frac{\pi^2}{6}.\qedhere
\]
\end{solution}

